hi i'm mark sorrells professor of plant  breeding genetics at cornell university  and i've been here since 1978 and  really have  enjoyed working here at cornell  my main responsibility here is to  develop new varieties of oatsweet and  barley for the northeastern u.s  focus of my research is on developing  new breeding methods  i'm going to start with a little bit of  information about our breeding program  here at cornell i have three technicians  david bencher is our research support  specialist  james tanaka is our lab technician and  josh connect is our field assistant  i also have a postdoc by the name of  lauren brazalski working on oat  metabolomics  i have several graduate students  dan sweeney just graduated and he's  currently breeding popcorn in in indiana  chitayama gursa just graduated and she's  now a durham wheat breeder in ethiopia  ellie toggin is from seattle  and she is currently on a six-month  internship with bayer crop science based  in st louis missouri  carl kunzi is from wyoming new york and  his uh project is to develop new  varieties of naked barley and winter  malting barley for the northeastern u.s  last and also travis rooney is from  college station texas  he's studying the impact of storage on  malting quality and seed dormancy in  pre-over sprouting resistant  spring malting barley  i have a new graduate student just just  joined us last week her name is kay  mcnary and she's from  missouri  she's currently working on the project  called value added grains for local and  regional food systems that i'll tell you  about more about in just a minute  [Applause]  cornell small grains project has a long  history  liberty high bailey established the  department of plant breeding in 1907  and  also  that same year dr h.j weber initiated  the small grains breeding project  dr harry hauser love came to the  department  as a graduate student in 1908  and in that following year he assumed  supervision of the small grains breeding  program  dr love expanded and continued to small  grains breeding at cornell university  until his retirement in 1949.  dr neil jensen came to cornell as a  graduate student in 1945  and he became the program leader in 1949  and myself i joined the project in 1978  and dr jensen retired in 1979.  since 1907 this breeding program has  released 34 winter wheat varieties  23 spring oat  seven winter  winter barley varieties  and four spring barley varieties  small grains are grown on more than  three hundred thousand acres in new york  and are valued at more than 1.5 million  dollars annually  our most recent variety releases are a  soft white winter wheat variety  that have very high levels of fusarium  headlight resistance a trait that's very  important for the northeast  we also released a spring malting barley  variety named excelsior gold  excelsior gold is the first spring  malting barley developed by cornell that  is well adapted to new york growing  conditions  we went from the first cross  in 2016  to certified seed production this year  this variety was developed in record  time  and is currently being grown for  certified seed production in this region  in the year 2012 new york state passed  the farm brewery law that gave tax and  marketing incentives  to breweries provided that they used at  least 60 percent of new york grown  ingredients in their in their brew  that stimulated the craft brewing  industry and today  new york state has the second most  breweries in the united states with more  than 440 licensed breweries  and they produce  more than a 1.2 million barrels of craft  beer annually  some of our currently funded projects  include  a project funded by the new world  foundation  on revitalizing grain production in the  hudson valley this is an important  project because it provides  organic vegetable growers in the hudson  valley  with options for rotating out of  vegetables into a high value food grain  new york state ag and markets supports  our research and extension for malting  barley production in this state  we also collaborate with jesse poland at  kansas state university on a usdaid  funded project  to develop high-yielding climate  resilient wheat varieties for south asia  using genomic selection  and then the brewers association is  funding a project titled impact of  storage on malting quality and seed  dormancy  in pre-over sprouting resistant spring  malting barley  we also have several usda funded  projects  including a project  funded by the  usda  wheat coordinated  agriculture project  that dr george debkowski at uc davis  leads  it's called validation and  characterization and deployment of qtl  for grain yield components in wheat we  also receive funding from the u.s  wheaton barley scab initiative to  develop  soft winter wheat varieties and spring  and winter barley varieties from the  northeastern u.s that have resistance if  usarium head blight  we collaborate with dr john lucianic  on a project  to  study the transcript transcriptomics and  metabolomics  and  to identify drivers of seed composition  and oat  pat hayes leads a project at oregon  state university  and we're involved in this project  titled multi-purpose organic naked  barley  elena vada manioc is in the crop science  department here at cornell and we work  with her on transcriptional regulation  of copper homeostasis in cereals and its  relationship to  fertility and grain yield  and then finally  we lead a usda organic research and  extension initiative project  titled value-added grains for local and  regional food systems  this project is focused on testing and  developing high-value food grains for  varieties in the northeast and midwest  growing regions  and it involves a collaboration with the  university of vermont  maine wisconsin illinois and south  dakota state university  so that gives you a little bit of an  overview about the small grains breeding  project at cornell university i hope to  see you around at the meetings and feel  free to contact me at any time with  questions or if you just want to chat  about small grains thank you very much  for listening today  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu
 

